#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "c5e60742-2571-5100-96be-a7578e6dda07" :question/math-academy-id "e-21540" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n![](images/ea/ea1c45c30bc57d8f04e7bba3e30a33884899fffd0fb712032595ee742a72704c.svg)\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n![](images/c3/c3be7834b927a4b8ba684899ee4e617a752ca3f5e09f10a320552fc7f497fb32.svg)\nFind the general solution of the system." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n![](images/6c/6c0f164deb5815fc1c79e3dd724b6725168213938a81089867f4343cb4f05974.svg)\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n![](images/5d/5dc5186154c40546a088db1890991e83c6f594d0042866ae9b86cddb3f2d08d8.svg)\nwhere $a,b$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of $\\mathbf{x}_p$ with respect to $t$ gives\n![](images/fa/fad28fb44ef22f703cbc8bd6a07d7d35e4a77ffe4fb3cd0ec5fdd3b107317a1b.svg)\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute $\\mathbf{x}_p$ and $\\mathbf{x}'_p$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 7[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a+b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a+b-1 \\\\ b-3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a+b-1=7a \\\\ b-3=7b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nb - 7b = 3\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nb = -\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n3a - \\dfrac12 - 1 = 7a\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na = -\\dfrac38.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](images/29/29dcb07dde2cdecb330fddb6168319527336ed7a217506727aa6c28241e32a4c.svg)\n\nAnd thus, the general solution is given by\n![](images/3e/3e1b5e881fb3c6d5688fd4978ed0fcc16aec5c6e105c11b919a6a9810762d698.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "6f03c3e2-26dc-5ef3-90d9-9b80feac1ad4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21541" :question/problem "![](images/19/192b8b3ce80dafb1a2db21822dad5b477cef90793305284210797cba32536a9f.svg)\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n![](images/55/55f17316cc2c0c5965b1d01598bceec9c2c472d6f6c949a099e65b778e6659e4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n![](images/e6/e6793e6bbadc20520b14292e048050c850340a535d2ade61a84d5235884794fa.svg)\nwhere $c_1,c_2$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2.$ Substituting $t = 0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\ {e}^{0}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-0+4 \\\\ -{0}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+4 \\\\ {c}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+4=6 \\\\ {c}_{2}+2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $c_1 = 2.$ From the second equation, we have $c_2 = -1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n![](images/bc/bcd9c686f0b6924614b0fadf4d86f7872f3fa33837aedfa0f89c15e9720a8f45.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "a5c44ae4-e55b-512f-ac1c-46f62950c1f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21543" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n![](images/ee/ee9461106f5c5b7de1ab9871852a1dcc5042947b635ffe10aba17286dac7124a.svg)\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n![](images/90/906d3f915ddca1fae48aa190a0a84236a91b50d5760db43816b1b72f20a4c52c.svg)\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t),$ where $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n![](images/97/9790c520e0752c38ac78a0b0266f7b6da292e4db16a353f34a902b587a4e688c.svg)\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n![](images/3d/3db9994bc0cab42e973fae4fa0b6f94ce880aa052707d11826fe94f6f75095ad.svg)\nwhere $a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51834713-edc2-5af3-a0dc-fb54ea4040c6" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "aabc0f35-fdfc-573a-8d86-ada823a380a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21544" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+4{t}^{2}+t-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the system of linear ODEs in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+4\\cdot {t}^{2}+1\\cdot t+(-2) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+0\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{2}+5\\cdot t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n![](images/4d/4dd9b46275644b83a7aa7ab9f9eaf14a8792442e07c4c4fd3a84a296f1fc2ccd.svg)\nwhere ![](images/96/96e96bf02a2ba8f46452e9ef33bcc6215b1b0696c4e1c6262c1400215857839a.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b602ede-d6e3-5993-b5d4-5dd360388fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b602ede-d6e3-5993-b5d4-5dd360388fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302635} :answer-field/id #uuid "59eea806-68ce-581f-9471-64557b35f190" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a07a6ab-6116-592a-a4a5-15923035ed8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a07a6ab-6116-592a-a4a5-15923035ed8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414510} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbafc658-89fb-5435-88ef-ee424c7542b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ffa37f0-fb51-548b-8827-5b9bd6774f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-329230" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -2[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2a \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 4a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2a \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-b+1 \\\\ 4a-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-b+1=-2a \\\\ 4a-2=-2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nb=2a+1.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n4a-2=-2(2a+1)\\,⇒\\,a=0.\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\nb=2(0)+1=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51834713-edc2-5af3-a0dc-fb54ea4040c6" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e977f63-9000-5403-a09e-c927ae18dc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e977f63-9000-5403-a09e-c927ae18dc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414511} :answer-field/id #uuid "5246fc34-6ab2-5f9f-b9da-e96c832c47b6" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414522} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd1ea2e-80e6-5727-a974-814275f13130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dd1ea2e-80e6-5727-a974-814275f13130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414499} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f29fb48-ed4b-52ad-b77b-99f62ab2bfc4" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414523} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0db643d-7d55-525a-b656-d6ea6465b9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0db643d-7d55-525a-b656-d6ea6465b9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ide